[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I reproduced end-to-end connectivity and return-path behavior and found the network operating normally. From pc1 to pc2 and back, ICMP reachability is 0% loss both directions with ~2 ms RTTs, so replies are not being dropped in the path. Routing on both hosts is correct (pc1 default via 195.11.14.1; pc2 default via 200.1.1.1). On the routers, BGP is established across the interconnect (193.10.11.0/24). Router1’s RIB contains 200.1.1.0/24 via 193.10.11.2 (BGP), and router2’s RIB contains 195.11.14.0/24 via 193.10.11.1 (BGP). “show ip bgp” on both routers confirms each side is advertising and receiving the partner LAN prefix, ensuring symmetric return routing across the peering link.

Mechanism-wise, with both prefixes installed via BGP and correct host defaults, replies from the partner LAN follow the same inter-router path back, so there is no reverse-path blackhole within this topology. Localization tests (pc1<->pc2 pings) traverse router1–router2 and confirm bidirectional forwarding.

Root cause: No network-side fault was found; the reported one-way behavior was not reproducible in the current state and may have been transient or application/host-firewall related outside the routers.

Corrective fix: No change needed on the routers. Recommend: verify the specific application flow on the endpoints (host firewalls/NAT, ports), and monitor BGP session stability and interface errors; engage if the issue recurs with timestamps/flows to correlate. 
=== END ===